---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const research = "/people/amin-samkough/research";
---

<PageLayout title="More info">
  <section id="about">
    <h2>About this site</h2>
    <p>
      This site collects information about the Samkough family. For Amin
      Samkough it includes a biography, family and archive photographs, and a
      <a href={`${research}/`}>research section</a> of articles, dictionary
      entries and one newspaper page that mention him. Most of those were
      written in Russian, Arabic or Turkish, so most of them have been
      translated into English for this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page explains how those translations were made and how much weight
      to give them.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="who">
    <h2>Who does the translating</h2>
    <p>
      The translations are made by an <strong>AI assistant</strong> working for
      the site’s owner, not by a professional translator or a native speaker. The
      owner picks the sources and supplies a web address, an image or pasted
      text. The assistant then reads the original, translates it, researches the
      date and the background, and writes the page.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Written by the assistant:</strong> the translations and
        summaries of the web articles, the dictionary entry, the Marj
        newspaper page, the Wikipedia page, and the captions on the archive
        photographs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Supplied by others:</strong> the first English version of the
        Mirzoev article (the
        <a href={`${research}/circassian-school-in-syria/`}>first translation</a>)
        was supplied to the site and its translator isn’t named. The
        <a href="/people/amin-samkough/homeland-poem/">homeland poem</a> was
        translated from Arabic by Adel Bashqawi.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>None of this has been checked by a fluent reader yet.</strong>
      Treat every translation as a careful working draft. For anything that
      matters, such as a date, a name or a quotation, check the original, which
      each page links to.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="process">
    <h2>How each page is made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The source is read.</strong> The assistant fetches the original
        web page, or reads the image or PDF. If a site blocks automated
        reading, that block is respected and the owner is asked for the text or
        the images instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is translated.</strong> The aim is to stay close to the
        original wording, including awkward phrasing, and to flag what is
        unclear instead of smoothing it over.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dates and background are checked.</strong> The page records
        where the text appeared and when (see
        <a href="#dates">Dates</a>), and compares its claims with the other
        sources on the site.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Doubtful points are marked.</strong> See
        <a href="#review">Highlighted “Review” text</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The page is filed</strong> in the research section under the
        language of the original (Russian, Arabic or Turkish).
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Every research page starts with an “About this page” box giving the
      title, the author where known, the <strong>published date</strong>, the
      <strong>source link</strong> and the language.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="full-or-summary">
    <h2>Full translations, summaries and quotes</h2>
    <p>
      Most of the articles are other people’s published work. Rather than
      reproduce them, most pages give a <strong>summary in the assistant’s own
      words, a few short translated passages, and a list of things to check</strong>,
      with a link to the original. The main exceptions are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={`${research}/circassian-school-in-syria-full-translation/`}>The Mirzoev article</a>,
        which is translated in full, with numbered notes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${research}/arabic-wikipedia-amin-ayyub-samkoug/`}>The Arabic Wikipedia entry</a>,
        translated in full because Wikipedia’s text is freely licensed (CC
        BY-SA). The page credits the authors and is shared under the same
        license.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${research}/marj-newspaper/`}>The Marj newspaper page</a> and
        the dictionary entry, which are short and are translated in full.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="review">
    <h2>Highlighted “Review” text</h2>
    <p>
      Where something is doubtful, the page marks it, usually with the label
      <mark>Review</mark>, and says why. A passage is flagged when:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the original is unclear, damaged or hard to read;</li>
      <li>a word or name can be translated more than one way;</li>
      <li>a claim disagrees with another source on this site;</li>
      <li>a claim has no source, or reads like praise instead of record;</li>
      <li>a date is missing, approximate or doesn’t add up.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The Marj page also gives each line a confidence rating (High, Medium or
      Low), and the full Mirzoev translation numbers its doubtful passages
      (†1, †2 and so on) with notes at the end. A passage that isn’t marked is
      not guaranteed to be right. It only means the assistant had no specific
      doubt.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="dates">
    <h2>Dates</h2>
    <p>
      Each research page gives a published date, found in this order of
      preference:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>a date printed on the page or in the article;</li>
      <li>a date in the page’s code, or in the site’s public data;</li>
      <li>
        the earliest copy in the Internet Archive, shown as “undated, archived
        by …” and never as a publication date;
      </li>
      <li>a date from the page history, for a wiki.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A date on a web page is usually when <em>that copy</em> was posted. It is
      not always when the text was written, and it can be changed afterward. So
      pages say when the date might be late or unreliable. For example, one
      profile’s site data gave 2020, but the archived copy showed it was posted
      in 2019, so the page uses
      <a href={`${research}/circassian-center-emin-semsug/`}>2019</a>. Another
      is <a href={`${research}/biyografi-net-emin-semsug/`}>undated</a>, and
      the page says so.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="independence">
    <h2>Sources that copy each other</h2>
    <p>
      Many of these pages are not independent. Several Turkish profiles repeat
      one text that goes back to 2009, an Arabic blog repeats an Arabic
      article, and a Turkish article is a translation of the Russian one. Each
      page says when it is a copy, and what it adds or changes. Please don’t
      count two copies as two confirmations. Several of them seem to trace back
      to a 2007 book, which I haven’t seen.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="conflicts">
    <h2>When sources disagree</h2>
    <p>
      The sources disagree about basic facts, and the site does not pick a
      winner. For example, his birth year is given as 1900 in some and 1903 in
      others, his death as 1952 or 1953, and the years he was mayor, a member
      of parliament, and when the school opened all differ. Each page lists its
      conflicts with the others. If you need one figure, treat these as open
      questions until a primary document turns up.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="names">
    <h2>Spelling of names</h2>
    <p>
      His name appears in many spellings because it passes through different
      alphabets: <em>Samgug</em> (from Russian), <em>Samkough</em> (the spelling
      used for this site), <em>Samkoug</em> (from Arabic), and <em>Semsuğ</em>
      or <em>Semguğ</em> (Turkish). The first name is <em>Amin</em> in Arabic
      and Russian, and <em>Emin</em> in Turkish. Pages keep the spelling used
      by their source and say so, and place and organization names are
      transliterated in the same way.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Machine translation can be wrong in ways that read smoothly,
        particularly for names, titles, dates, idioms and the older Ottoman
        Turkish.
      </li>
      <li>
        Handwriting on the photographs, and the printed text of the Marj page,
        were read from images and may contain misreadings.
      </li>
      <li>
        Historical claims in the sources have mostly not been checked against
        archives. The pages compare sources with each other but don’t settle
        who is right.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pages are updated when better evidence turns up, so a date or a
        conclusion may change.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Back to the <a href={`${research}/`}>research section</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
